    "durationMs": 305457,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and verified. \n\n**What I built:**\n- **Persistence**: Spring Data JPA entity (`Link`) + repository backed by PostgreSQL, with a Flyway migration (`V1__create_links_table.sql`) that creates the `links` table with a unique `code` constraint. Schema survives restarts; `ddl-auto=none` so Flyway owns the schema.\n- **API**: `POST /links` (201 with generated or custom 4-20 char code, 409 on duplicate, 422 on bad url/code/malformed JSON), `GET /links/{code}`, `GET /r/{code}` (302 + atomic `clicks + 1` JPQL update, exactly once per request), and `GET /links` (ascending creation order with `total`).\n- **Config**: An `EnvironmentPostProcessor` parses `DATABASE_URL` (postgres:// or postgresql://, percent-encoding aware) into JDBC properties — no hard-coded credentials.\n- **start.sh**: execs the prebuilt fat jar (`target/app.jar`) in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`; Flyway applies pending migrations during startup before the API serves. An offline Maven rebuild is the fallback if the jar is missing.\n\n**Verification**: 34/34 smoke checks passed (creation, duplicate conflicts, all 422 validation cases, redirects with exact Location headers, click increments, listing order). I also restarted the server mid-test and confirmed click counts persisted, then re-verified a clean `sh start.sh` cycle and left the database empty for the test suite. The README documents how to start the server."
  }
